r from enhanced recovery  from existing fields and of course most  of that's likely to be found outside the  continental united states  the u.s is the world's biggest consumer  of petroleum and petroleum products an  adequate supply of reasonably priced oil  is clearly absolutely vital to the  american economy and yet most of the  world's oil reserves some 97  are under the control of foreign  governments  for the rest of the world including half  of the world's population currently  making the transition from planned  economies to free markets energy will  clearly be the critical building block  and that's transformation  today international oil and gas  companies have plenty of capital but are  presented with a limited number of  opportunities  in new areas providing the scale and the  return attractive to the industry  most of the countries with really  sizable oil reserves are still closed  including much of the middle east saudi  arabia kuwait iraq iran for the most  part  opportunities in russia are on hold  mexico is closed and venezuela only  recently opened up  other areas are mature such as the north  sea the u.s and indonesia  so the caspian region with truly vast  undeveloped oil and gas resources  represents new investment opportunities  for companies  in an otherwise opportunity constrained  world  of course with opportunity come  obstacles problems and risk many to do  with the transportation of oil markets  still development of caspian oil ought  to be good for the countries involved  the investing companies and for world  oil supplies so it's worth the effort  obviously for us to try to find  solutions  my experience and perspective on  geopolitics  and energy tell me one thing very  clearly  the caspian region is of enormous  significance to the future of a  diversified and balanced global oil  market  in the coming years as global oil demand  grows the world will need new sources  the caspian has great hydrocarbon  potential to meet those needs but the  realization of this potential depends  not only on commercial attractiveness  but also on politics  as the struggle to get these resources  to market involves numerous countries  with competing interest  howard geopolitics shaping the events in  the region since the collapse of the  soviet union world competition has  shifted from geopolitical and military  power to economic and commercial  competition  this is reflected in economic  competition in regions around the globe  the caspian is on the front lines of  this global competition as nations and  commercial interests now jockey for  influence  over the caspian region's vast oil and  gas resources and the access routes to  markets  while the newly independent nations face  the challenges of statehood  outside countries are still vying for  influence and domination  the power game is being played out  through pipeline routes and commercial  competition  i won't delve into the specifics of  pipeline roots and security i know  you've spent a lot of time on that  already today but i do want to speak  about some of the broader political  issues and the primary interests  of the main international players  aside from of course the countries  themselves  the main players clearly are russia  turkey iran china and the united states  each group has distinct concerns and  motivations  russia looms large over the entire  region by virtue of its proximity  and history of soviet control over these  countries  as secretary of defense i used to think  about this region of the world as a  target  um now as the ceo of an energy service  company obviously i have a slightly  different perspective  but there's to some extent the target  analogy is still appropriate because of  the way in which the the rest of the  world is sort of zeroed in on what is a  clearly vital piece of real estate  with its near abroad policy russia has  attempted to assert continuing influence  over these countries by applying  pressure in numerous ways such as  pushing them to join the commonwealth of  independent states  russia has also sought military bases on  many of these territories some would say  that moscow wants to keep the newly  independent states weak  by limiting oil exports with a fallback  position of ensuring  that if caspian oil does flow it goes  through russian territory  with constrained export capacity it does  not make sense for russia to back out  its own crude oil exports in exchange  for transit fees from caspian oil  exports  caspian area crude is likely to move  through russian pipelines when it suits  the russians interest  my view is that russia ultimately wants  to maximize its economic gains from the  leverage it has over these newly  independent states  overall russia is concerned about  setting precedence in dealing with a  variety of issues with the newly  independent states particularly about  the exploitation of natural resources  they want credit for any economic or  energy development that took place when  these countries were soviet republics  moscow wants to establish its rights  early even if it means slowing  development part of this is reflected in  their position on the lake versus c  legal status of the caspian  the bottom line in my mind russia wants  to maintain political leverage in the  near abroad  turkey another important player clearly  has some goals similar to russia but  also some of its own unique concerns  like russia turkey would like political  leverage in the caspian region and wants  to maximize the economic benefits that  would evolve from that leverage  turkish cultural influence over newly  independent countries in the regions has  been somewhat limited and as investors  they have limited capital  however they obviously would like to  play a key role as an export route  favoring a pipeline pipeline route  through turkey devoid prosperous oil  tanker traffic  the risk to the turks is an alternate  pipeline route out of the black sea such  as one through bulgaria and greece  turkey has a dynamic private sector and  turkish interest would also like to  broaden key commercial alliances and  diversify sources of oil and gas  supplies to meet their own rising energy  needs  complicating turkish concerns and  pipeline routes are issues of kurdish  insurgency the armenian azerbaijani  conflict and deep suspicions and  relations between russia and greece  a broader concern for ankara and the  post-soviet cold war era is simply to  maintain geopolitical significance with  the west  iran is another story but like russia  and turkey it also desires political  leverage and influence in the casper and  region  iran would like to establish key  commercial alliances and share in the  economic benefits of developing caspian  resources iran's proximity provides the  most direct exit route for certain oil  and gas exports if pipelines could be  constructed across its territory  asian demand may provide a better market  than the mediterranean for caspian oil  iran has apparently now moved in favor  of carving up the caspian rights among  the littoral states instead of a 45 mile  limit  after the breakup of the soviet union  and the creation of the newly  independent states it's fair to say  that iran's potential influence has been  heightened in this region  now china is also becoming a player in  the region i was in china just a few  weeks ago to have  a session with the head of the cnpc the  chinese national petroleum corporation  and he wanted to talk about kazakhstan  and cnpc's investments there as well as  in other parts of the world  china's goals are clearly both economic  and political chinese influence is also  seen through the aggressive positioning  of cnpc in the uz and acute vinsk  projects in kazakhstan there's the  potential for a substantial movement of  oil and gas through pipelines which  could stretch across kazakhstan an  additional exit route for energy  which goes neither to the west nor  through the stra