rong viable  democratic states  these nations are being transformed from  being part of the soviet hinterland to  being free societies  it is in the u s interest to see them  enjoy peace and prosperity  a high standard of living and to become  countries that can contribute to the  community of nations  developing the economies and the natural  resources of this region are a key part  of this process and i think also  obviously a strategic interest to the  united states  however the administration's policy  toward the caspian region is strongly  affected  by our middle east policy specifically  the notion of dual containment of iraq  and iran  some observers would point out that the  unintended result of the policy towards  iran  is to give russia even more leverage  over the independent states of central  asia and the caucuses by blocking export  routes south  out of iran  thus our policy towards iran contradicts  our policy toward encouraging  independence for the states and the  caspian region  as a broader policy the u.s also wants  to see more diversity in the sources of  the world's oil supply and to encourage  opportunities for u.s companies and  commercial interests  although middle east countries including  iraq still have  vast undeveloped oil reserves and  potential it is in our strategic and our  economic interest to encourage the  opening up of new areas of supply  this can create a more balanced and less  vulnerable world market  so what is needed to ensure the  independence and the development of  these countries independence requires  economic strength and i believe this  will come from a free market foundation  hand in hand with democracy  the republics need to establish their  own independent sources of wealth to  finance this economic and political  independence one of the main drivers of  economic growth and independence clearly  will be the development of their oil and  gas resources  a crucial element in the caspian's  economic development is the urgency  of receiving the benefits for developing  these natural resources  the time it will take  for significant exports of oil to begin  and then for oil companies to recover  their cost could mean that the local  economies and people will not see  direct benefits for some time  of course there may be secondary  benefits from jobs and infrastructure  it's important for these new governments  to show financial and economic gains in  the short run  if they're going to keep their countries  on track  while oil may be a key driver i think  diversity of the economic base is also  important this applies to diversity  among foreign investors big and small as  well  infrastructure is clearly another  crucial component to economic growth and  this goes beyond just oil pipelines  building the infrastructure and  developing the oil and gas resources  will clearly require massive foreign  investment  what is needed to create an attractive  investment climate there clearly are a  range of political problems that need to  be solved mostly interrelated with  export pipeline routes one key issue to  be resolved of course is the offshore  boundaries the lake versus sea dispute  is still a major impediment to foreign  investment and a key element for foreign  investment and economic growth is peace  in the region  that should be pursued through both  bilateral and multilateral channels  there also needs to be stability in the  application of the rule of law including  a reliable framework for production  sharing contracts the nations must  recognize they are in competition with  the rest of the world their investment  climate must be attractive and i believe  free markets are a key it is important  there be a level playing field for  developing the energy resources the  caspian  cooperation and competition among  companies should be based on good  business not on outside government  pressure or spheres of influence  in other words the development should be  approached on an economic  rather than on a geopolitical basis  this is all part of free and open  markets  economic interdependence should be  regarded as a positive force countries  are often concerned about being  dependent on their neighbors but  economic links can bind the interest of  countries together  cross-border investments and shared  infrastructure like pipelines can lead  to greater stability by hard hard-wiring  relationships solving the problems  involved in developing the great  resources of the caspian region will  require a lot of cooperative effort  companies will have to work together  with each other and with multiple  governments  success in developing the oil and gas  resources in the casper and the rest of  the world for that matter depend on the  degree to which market forces are  permitted to work  market forces are transforming the world  and energy is the crucial building block  we would not enjoy the enormous peace  and prosperity and opportunity that we  enjoy at the end of the 20th century if  it weren't for the enormous impact of  market forces in the global economy over  the last several decades  wrong-headed policies can interfere with  the free market with disastrous results  the white house and congress share the  blame  in my mind for misguided policies like  wage price controls back in the 70s  and the sanctions on azerbaijan iran and  other nations imposed for essentially  domestic political reasons  when prices are relatively low  politicians are less concerned about  long-term supply security and  vulnerability and they feel free to do  things such as using oil sanctions as a  foreign policy weapon  the oil industry makes large long-term  capital investments in fixed assets in  developing countries all over the globe  the long-term horizon is often at odds  with the pressures of short-term  political issues  companies must write out changes in  governments and policies and shifting  diplomatic relations  the us government needs to extend its  horizon as well and think about  think about the longer term strategic  interest that are involved  i'm often asked what it's like going  from being secretary of defense to  becoming chairman and ceo at halliburt i  explained there are a lot of  similarities  the biggest problem i faced as secretary  of defense was the united states  congress  now that i'm chairman of ceo at  halliburton the biggest problem i face  is the united states congress  another question i'm often asked since  halliburton operates in about 120  countries around the world is what about  political risk isn't it dangerous to to  function in that many places don't you  run into problems  with political risk various places  what i find is that while we operate in  some pretty hairy places we're far more  likely to be shut down in a country by  developments in washington than by  anything that's ever happened to us  overseas the biggest political risk we  face as a company and as an industry  is in fact right here in washington dc  on capitol hill  the fact is the u.s oil industry is an  invaluable strategic asset for the  united states congress needs to  understand that and unfortunately they  don't  it may well be that the average oil  executive knows more about foreign  policy than the average congressman with  the cold war over congress is now  dominated by domestic concerns and  domestic interest groups  i heard a disturbing statistic the other  day it was that uh  of our freshmen  who now arrive in washington to begin  their service in the u.s congress 30  percent of them arrive without passports  30 percent have never been outside the  continental united states  there is a silver lining in all of this  it's comforting to note that one of the  institutions held an even lower esteem  than the oil industry is the us  congress i actually moved up to a more  prestigious profession when i gave up  politics  and became part of the industry  let me turn for just a moment if i can  to the question of 