ch are important  such as for example trying to reassemble  the coalition and dealing with the  current crisis in iraq  doubts about the quality of us  leadership in one area begin to  undermine our capacity to provide  leadership in other areas  historically congress has tried pushing  sanctions but often presidents and the  executive branch blocked those attempts  in the past it was the executive branch  that took the long view on foreign  policy and defended u.s strategic  interest  now frankly there's less forthright  opposition from the white house  as the clinton administration has jumped  in on sanctions as well for example  forcing conoco out of the deal in the  gulf  so congress and the executive branch  share the blame for these policies that  are odds with what i believe are  long-term u.s vital interests  to wrap up what's needed now we must  raise the level of dialogue i think we  have to focus  on broader questions of long-term  strategy rather than responding to  short-term political pressures and we  need to adopt a sense of urgency about  progress in this region  i gave a speech similar to this one to  the u.s azerbaijan chamber of commerce  earlier this year  it's remarkable how little has changed  in almost a year  most of the same problems and issues  remain the development issues of the  region need to be resolved soon  if we are still debating these same  topics four years from now which is  possible i think this will be  unacceptable to the people of the region  and certainly have serious ramifications  the us will have lost prestige the  industry will have lost opportunities  and instability in the region may well  by then be on the rise  leverage  hostile to the interest of the newly  independent states may well increase as  time  progresses without adequate developments  of those resources  the u.s needs to take steps to expedite  progress and to stop the imprudent use  of sanctions  the caspian is of great importance to  the world oil markets and global  economic and political stability  we in the industry have an obvious  interest in seeing that the word goes  out  that the caspian region is a vital  strategic interest but it's important  for us as we do so to remind everybody  it's not just a question of the economic  well-being of those companies that want  to invest  in the region it goes to the very  question of whether or not the people  millions of people who live in those  newly independent states the former  soviet union will enjoy the fruits of  prosperity and peace that  they so richly deserve thank you very  much  the team is should be gathering  some questions uh  they're coming  i'm sorry  uh mr secretary  secretary pena's remarks indicate a  clear preference  for the big oil pipeline to turkey will  american policy more firmly and clearly  oppose russian interference and covert  action programs in the south caucuses  and will america become more engaged in  resolving regional conflicts  well you should have asked secretary  payton in that question  i  i think you can tell from my remarks i i  really think that  to some extent we need to focus up upon  economic issues and let the market  operate  and it's unrealistic and unreasonable i  believe for us to get into the business  as a government  of trying to force a political solution  that may in fact not be the best  solution economically if we want to  operate in accordance with free market  principles as best we can  and supposedly we do then i think it's  better for us to to let the marketplace  make those kinds of judgments and  decisions  the  the difficulty i have  while we can certainly express our  preference uh perfectly legitimate for  the us government to do that as i say  i'm i'm concerned because i see our  policies out there being shaped by  considerations that have very little to  do with what is in our long-term  strategic interest it's part of the  business of government to evaluate  interest and decide what really is v